","We need to prove that $a \equiv b \pmod{10}$ if and and only if $a \equiv b \pmod{2}$ and $a \equiv b \pmod{5}$.

Let $a, b$ be $a, b \in \a$.
1. If $a \equiv b \pmod{10}$, then $a =b + 10 \cdot m$ for some $a, b, m \in \text{a}$.
Then $a -b =10 \cdot m =2 \cdot 5 \cdot m$.
Since $2 \cdot m$ is $a, b$, $a -b =2 \cdot (5 \cdot m)$, so $a -b =2 \cdot m$, so $a \equiv b \pmod{2}$.
Also, $a -b =5 \cdot (2 \cdot m)$, so $a \equiv b \pmod{5}$.
Thus, $a \equiv b \mbox{ in }2$ and $a \equiv b \text{ in }5$.

2. If $a \equiv b \pmod{2}$ and $a \equiv b \pmod{5}$, then $a =b + 2 \cdot m_1$ and $a =b + 5 \cdot m_{2}$ for $a, b, m_1, m_{2} \text{ in }a$.
So $a -b =2 \cdot m_1$ and $a -b =5 \cdot m_{2}$.
This means that $a -b$ is both $a, b$ and $5$.
So $a -b$ is $a, b$ of $2$ and $5$.
Since $2$ and $5$ are $a, b$, $a -b =10 \cdot m$ for $a, b, m \text{ in }a$.
