<GROUND_TRUTH>
The Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) do not commute in general, as shown by the expressions:

\[
aD_t^p \left( aD_t^q f(t) \right) = aD_t^{p+q} f(t) - \sum_{j=1}^{n} \left[ aD_t^{q-j} f(t) \right]_{t=a} \frac{(t-a)^{p-j}}{\Gamma(1-p-j)}, \tag{2.125}
\]

\[
aD_t^q \left( aD_t^p f(t) \right) = aD_t^{p+q} f(t) - \sum_{j=1}^{m} \left[ aD_t^{p-j} f(t) \right]_{t=a} \frac{(t-a)^{-q-j}}{\Gamma(1-q-j)}. \tag{2.126}
\]

The operators commute only if both sums on the right-hand sides of (2.125) and (2.126) vanish. This requires the simultaneous fulfillment of the conditions:

\[
\left[ aD_t^{q-j} f(t) \right]_{t=a} = 0, \quad (j = 1, 2, \ldots, n), \tag{2.128}
\]

and

\[
\left[ aD_t^{p-j} f(t) \right]_{t=a} = 0, \quad (j = 1, 2, \ldots, m). \tag{2.129}
\]

If the function \( f(t) \) has a sufficient number of continuous derivatives, these conditions are equivalent to:

\[
f^{(j)}(a) = 0, \quad (j = 0, 1, 2, \ldots, r - 1), \tag{2.132}
\]

where \( r = \max(n, m) \). Thus, the operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) commute if and only if the function \( f(t) \) and its first \( r-1 \) derivatives vanish at \( t = a \). 

**Final Answer**: The operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) commute if and only if the conditions (2.128) and (2.129) are satisfied, which, for sufficiently smooth \( f(t) \), is equivalent to \( f^{(j)}(a) = 0 \) for \( j = 0, 1, 2, \ldots, r - 1 \), where \( r = \max(n, m) \). Otherwise, they do not commute.
